yes Kimberly the government has been  facing questions about whether it acted  too slowly to tackle this crisis so this  is where we are today you can see 596  deaths now we often see a lag on the  weekends the numbers can be slightly  lower but this is the lowest number we  have seen now for nearly two weeks and  if we look at how our figures compared  to other countries you can see we are  following in that similar trajectory to  countries like Italy and France now the  focus today has been on what Britain was  doing much earlier in the outbreak well  before lockdown at a time when it seemed  the virus was mainly a Chinese problem  so let's take this same chart back to  that first government Cobra meeting on  the 24th of January at this point China  had put Wuhan into lockdown 18 people  you can see that line there representing  the number of deaths in China 18 people  who died in the country on the same day  Europe reported its first cases in  France the UK would see its first  confirmed case a week later now Italy  locked down Lombardy on the 21st of  February a day later we would see the  first deaths there but by now the UK  government had set out that plan to  contain delay research and mitigate it  was nearly two weeks after that the  first person died in the UK and more  than a fortnight after that that we went  into lockdown here by then there were  three hundred and thirty five reported  hospital deaths and this is where we are  now it's more than 16,000 people have  died in hospital we've had time to  prepare we've seen the trajectory in  other countries but we're locking down  earlier have made a difference and there  isn't a simple right answer that says  you press this button and it will all go  away it's it's about managing the  competing of the deaths  you know tragic deaths on one hand with  the interventions on the other had we  got into this state of lockdown  mid-january yeah then then quite  possibly we would have had very very few  cases the government has always said its  decisions will be guided by the science  but some of those very scientists are  now warning that the threat of new  waves of the virus in the coming months  means it's too soon to say for sure  whether the right decisions were made at  the right time
 

